        "summary":  "Anthropic\u0027s life sciences research group used Claude AI agents to autonomously discover a previously unknown enzyme system called array-associated reverse transcriptases (ARTs) found in bacteriophages. After searching over 200,000 reverse transcriptases across 21 hours, Claude identified an unusual pattern of repeating DNA sequences resembling CRISPR architecture. Follow-up lab experiments confirmed the system\u0027s novel characteristics, demonstrating AI\u0027s capacity to generate testable scientific hypotheses at scale.",
        "execSummary":  "Claude autonomously scanned 200,000+ biological sequences over 21 hours and identified a genuinely novel enzyme system that human researchers had not previously characterized — a landmark demonstration of agentic AI performing original scientific discovery, not just literature synthesis. For enterprise leaders evaluating AI agents for knowledge-intensive work, this is the most concrete proof point yet that long-horizon autonomous research tasks are within reach for current-generation models. The implication for R\u0026D-intensive enterprises — pharma, materials science, cybersecurity — is that AI agents can compress hypothesis generation from months to hours at a fraction of the cost. The caveat: Anthropic is both developer and publisher of this result, so independent replication and peer review remain the bar for scientific credibility.",
