        "date":  "2026-09-16",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "The article identifies a Communication-Reasoning Gap in multi-agent AI systems, where agents exchange messages but fail to convert that interaction into effective collective computation, particularly as system complexity scales. Researchers propose an Internet of Cognition framework emphasizing shared intent, coordinated context, and negotiation mechanisms as the critical next layer of agent infrastructure. Human-agent hybrid systems require explainability and feedback loops to maintain accountability at scale.",
        "execSummary":  "The core finding is actionable: connectivity between agents does not produce coordination, and organizations scaling from a handful of agents to enterprise-wide deployment will hit this wall. The Communication-Reasoning Gap described here is the architectural problem underneath most reported failures in multi-agent customer service and operations deployments — agents talk past each other rather than toward a shared goal. CIOs should evaluate current multi-agent architectures for the presence of governance layers that resolve conflicts, maintain shared context, and provide human oversight — currently present in only a small minority of enterprise deployments. Treating agent deployment count as a maturity metric, without these coordination layers, is a reliable path to expensive, visible failures.",
