        "theme":  "Enterprise",
        "summary":  "KPMG\u0027s latest quarterly AI survey finds that organizations using AI extensively deploy advanced cybersecurity defenses at dramatically higher rates (58%) compared to companies in early experimentation phases (8%), while paradoxically reporting higher concern about security as an adoption barrier. Nearly 40% of respondents are building or using multiagent systems, and mature AI users allocate larger budgets to cybersecurity. Executive accountability for AI governance is identified as the key differentiator in organizations with effective security postures.",
        "execSummary":  "The KPMG data resolves a common executive debate: AI maturity and security maturity move together, not in tension — organizations that use AI most extensively are also best protected. The paradox is that mature AI users worry more about security, not less, because they understand the threat surface better; this is a sign of sophistication, not fragility. For customer-support and enterprise operations leaders, the 40% multiagent adoption figure and the correlation with increased security investment signals that agentic deployments are now mainstream enough to require formal security governance, not just IT review. The clearest action item from the survey: assign explicit executive ownership of AI security, as this single factor most strongly predicts effective governance.",
