        "date":  "2026-09-25",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "Two-thirds of organizations currently use AI agents in production and plan to expand deployment sixfold over the next year, yet only 29% of these agents interact with each other, and only 7% of enterprises have governance and orchestration capabilities in place. Enterprises plan to double agentic AI budgets despite lacking clear productivity evidence and proper technical foundations. The disconnect between investment enthusiasm and operational readiness is the central finding.",
        "execSummary":  "The most important number in this piece is 7% — the share of enterprises that have actual governance and orchestration capabilities for their agents — because it defines how exposed the other 93% are as they double their budgets. Organizations racing to deploy agents without coordination infrastructure are building what one researcher called a thousand instruments playing at once without a conductor. For customer-support leaders, the sixfold planned expansion of agent deployments over the next year makes orchestration architecture a board-level priority, not an IT implementation detail. The risk is not that agents fail individually — it is that uncoordinated agents create inconsistent customer experiences, compliance exposure, and unmeasurable cost structures that compound over time.",
